      highlights: {
        title: 'Wildlife Highlights',
        description: `Amboseli National Park is globally renowned for its massive, free-roaming elephant herds — among the best-studied on Earth — which move with ease across the open plains and wetland edges directly below Mount Kilimanjaro. The Enkiama and Longinye swamps, permanently fed by underground streams filtering through Kilimanjaro's volcanic soils, form the ecological heart of the park and attract huge concentrations of hippos, buffaloes, and waterbirds. Lions and cheetahs hunt openly on the short-grass plains, making for exceptional sightings, while leopards lurk less visibly along the swamp fringes and acacia thickets. Amboseli is also one of the finest birding parks in East Africa, with over 600 species including vast flocks of pelicans, flamingos, and bee-eaters.`
      },

      bestTime: {
        title: 'Best Time for Wildlife Viewing',
        description: `The best time for wildlife viewing in Amboseli is during the dry season from June to October and during the short dry spell of January and February. During these drier months, animals congregate predictably around the permanent swamps, making sightings dense and reliable. Elephant herds are particularly impressive as they move between the swamps and open plains throughout the day. The dry months also bring the clearest views of Kilimanjaro, especially in the early morning hours before cloud builds up. During the green season (November–May), the landscape is lush and photogenic, birdlife is exceptional with migratory arrivals, and predator–prey interactions around the swamp edges remain highly active.`
      },

