      highlights: {
        title: 'Wildlife Highlights',
        description: `Arabuko Sokoke is East Africa's largest coastal indigenous forest and one of the continent's most critical biodiversity hotspots. It is home to three species found almost nowhere else: the Aders' duiker (critically endangered), the Sokoke bushy-tailed mongoose (endangered), and the golden-rumped elephant shrew — one of Africa's most remarkable and improbably fast small mammals. Forest elephants and buffaloes move seasonally through the deeper zones. Suni antelope are occasionally seen on the forest floor. The reserve is, however, internationally renowned above all for birds, with three strict endemics (Clarke's weaver, Sokoke scops-owl, and Sokoke pipit) and an array of near-endemic coastal forest species making it the foremost birding forest in East Africa.`
      },

      bestTime: {
        title: 'Best Time for Wildlife Viewing',
        description: `The best time for wildlife viewing in Arabuko Sokoke depends on what you are seeking. For birds — the primary draw — October to April is optimal, when resident endemics are joined by Palearctic and intra-African migrants. Clarke's weaver is most reliably found near its breeding colonies from October to January. The Sokoke scops-owl can be located on guided night walks year-round but is most vocal and responsive from October to March. For mammals including elephants and the golden-rumped elephant shrew, activity is generally highest in the early mornings throughout the year, with the shrew most visible along the short trails near the forest headquarters.`
      },

