      wildlifePreview: {
        description: 'Hell\'s Gate is home to giraffes, plains zebras, buffaloes, Grant\'s gazelles, impalas, Defassa waterbucks, elands, warthogs, and olive baboons — all of which can be encountered on foot or by bicycle. While big cats are absent, the park\'s wildlife density and accessibility create an intimate, ground-level experience that is impossible to replicate in a vehicle. Lammergeier, Verreaux\'s eagle, and a rich community of cliff-nesting raptors make this one of Kenya\'s finest sites for large raptor observation.',
        linkText: 'More about the wildlife'
      },

      thingsToDoPreview: [
        {
          description: 'Cycling through the main valley among free-roaming wildlife is Hell\'s Gate\'s signature experience and one of the most joyful things you can do in Kenya. The dramatic Lower and Upper Gorges offer guided hiking through narrow rock chimneys, over boulders, and through the Njorowe Gorge\'s natural hot spring pools. Rock climbing on Fischer\'s Tower and the Central Tower provides technical challenges on excellent volcanic rock. Day trips from Nairobi are easy; combining with a Lake Naivasha boat safari and hippo-watching makes a perfect two-day Naivasha itinerary.'
        }
      ],

      weatherPreview: {
        description: 'Hell\'s Gate sits at 1,890m in the Great Rift Valley near Lake Naivasha, giving it a cool, pleasant highland climate. Temperatures range from 12°C to 26°C (54°F to 79°F) year-round. The park is enjoyable in any season, though the dry months of June to October and January to February offer the driest cycling and gorge-walking conditions. Rainfall is moderate and typically falls in short afternoon showers during the wet season.',
        linkText: 'More about the weather and climate'
      },

      bestTimePreview: {
        description: 'Hell\'s Gate can be visited year-round from Nairobi, but the best conditions for cycling and gorge walking are during the dry seasons of June to October and January to February. Weekday visits are significantly more peaceful than weekends. The gorge walk requires some wading through shallow water in wet conditions — check water levels before visiting during the long rains (April–May).',
        linkText: 'More about the best time to visit'
      },
