      malariaRisk: {
        title: 'Malaria Risk',
        description: 'Hell\'s Gate\'s altitude of approximately 1,890m places it in a low malaria risk zone. Antimalarial prophylaxis is generally not considered necessary for short visits to this altitude, but consult your travel health provider for personalised advice based on your full Kenya itinerary.'
      },
      wildlifeViewing: {
        title: 'Safety When Cycling & Walking Among Wildlife',
        description: 'While Hell\'s Gate has no big predators, some of its wildlife — particularly buffalo — can be dangerous if approached too closely. Follow these guidelines for safe cycling and gorge walking.',
        rules: [
          'Always hire a licensed KWS guide for the Njorowe Gorge walk — it is a KWS requirement and ensures safety and enjoyment.',
          'Never enter the gorge during or immediately after heavy rain — flash flooding can be extremely dangerous.',
          'When cycling near buffalo, maintain a safe distance and do not dismount between a buffalo and its group.',
          'Warthogs and zebras are generally not dangerous, but never approach or attempt to feed any wildlife.',
          'Wear a helmet when cycling — helmets are available for hire at the gate.',
          'Bring plenty of water — the cycling circuit can take 4–5 hours and sun exposure at Rift Valley altitude can cause dehydration.',
          'Wear sunscreen and a hat — UV exposure at 1,890m is higher than at sea level.',
          'For rock climbing on Fischer\'s Tower, use appropriate climbing equipment and ideally climb with a registered guide who knows the volcanic rock characteristics.'
        ]
      },
