      malariaRisk: {
        title: 'Malaria Risk',
        description: 'Kora National Park is at high malaria risk throughout the year. The hot, humid Tana River valley creates ideal conditions for Anopheles mosquitoes year-round. Antimalarial prophylaxis is essential for all visitors. Full personal protection measures — DEET repellent, long sleeves after dusk, mosquito net — are non-negotiable in this environment. Risk is highest immediately after the rains when standing water increases mosquito breeding.'
      },
      wildlifeViewing: {
        title: 'Wildlife Safety in a Remote Wilderness',
        description: 'Kora\'s lions, elephants, buffaloes, and Nile crocodiles all pose serious risks if standard protocols are not followed. All activities at Kora require armed ranger accompaniment.',
        rules: [
          'Never leave the vehicle or camp area without an armed KWS ranger — Kora\'s lions and elephants are genuinely wild and potentially dangerous.',
          'Do not approach the Tana River banks alone at any time — Nile crocodiles are present in all river sections.',
          'Never walk outside camp boundaries after dark — large predators are active at night throughout the park.',
          'Carry and drink at least 3–4 litres of water during any game drive or activity in the dry-season heat.',
          'Ensure your operator has satellite communication and an emergency medical evacuation plan before departure.',
          'Inform your guide or ranger immediately of any medical symptoms during your stay.',
          'Wear full sun protection at all times — Kora\'s low altitude and intense sun require high-SPF sunscreen, a hat, and long sleeves.',
          'Cover all skin and apply DEET repellent from dusk; Kora\'s mosquito population is among the most intense in Kenya.'
        ]
      },
