      wildlifePreview: {
        description: 'Lake Nakuru National Park protects one of Kenya\'s highest densities of large mammals in its 188km² fenced area. Both black and white rhino are well established — white rhino graze the open grassland flats while black rhino inhabit the euphorbia forest and dense woodland. Lion prides are resident and active throughout the park; several large leopards use the yellow fever woodland along the lake\'s western shore. Rothschild\'s giraffe — among the tallest and most endangered giraffe subspecies — are commonly seen against the lake backdrop. Hippo pools on the lake shore and large buffalo herds in the highland grasslands complete an extraordinarily rich wildlife community for a park of this size.',
        linkText: 'More about the wildlife'
      },

      thingsToDoPreview: [
        {
          description: 'Morning and late-afternoon game drives are the core activities at Lake Nakuru National Park, targeting the lake shore for rhino and hippo, the yellow fever woodland for leopard, and the highland grasslands for lions, buffaloes, and the park\'s resident Rothschild\'s giraffe. The Baboon Cliff viewpoint provides panoramic views over the lake and park from the escarpment edge — particularly spectacular when flamingos are present. Walking on the escarpment with a ranger, birding along the lake shore, and photography sessions at the pelican and cormorant colonies on the lake\'s rock outcrops complete a full day\'s activity programme.'
        }
      ],

      weatherPreview: {
        description: 'Lake Nakuru sits at 1,754–2,073m elevation in the Rift Valley and has a cool, high-altitude climate. Temperatures range from 10°C to 28°C (50°F to 82°F) year-round, with cool nights and mornings throughout the year. Two rainy seasons apply: long rains from March to May and short rains from October to November. The dry season from July to September and December to February are the best visiting periods.',
        linkText: 'More about the weather and climate'
      },

