        description: `Lewa Wildlife Conservancy is the single most important site on Earth for Grevy's zebra — a species classified as Endangered with fewer than 3,000 individuals remaining globally, more than 15% of which live on Lewa. The conservancy's rhino programme, established in 1983, has grown to include one of Kenya's largest black rhino sanctuaries and a thriving white rhino population, both reliably encountered on morning and afternoon drives. African wild dogs — one of Africa's most endangered large predators — have established resident packs on Lewa in recent years, and pack sightings are now a regular feature of Lewa game drives. The full northern Kenya Big Five — elephant, lion, leopard, buffalo, and both rhino species — are all present. Reticulated giraffe, Beisa oryx, Günther\'s dik-dik, gerenuk, and Jackson\'s hartebeest complete a fauna that represents the finest cross-section of northern Kenya wildlife in any single conservancy.`
