        title: 'Wildlife Highlights — Elephants, Colobus & the Forest Island',
        description: `Marsabit National Park's most celebrated wildlife is its elephant population — large-bodied, long-tusked bulls for which Marsabit has been renowned since the era of Ahmed, the legendary tusker who became the first animal in Kenya to receive presidential protection in the 1970s. Today's Marsabit elephants carry some of the finest remaining ivory in East Africa, and close encounters with great bulls in the forest are an experience of unforgettable impact. Black-and-white colobus monkeys inhabit the dense montane forest canopy, providing easy and striking viewing. Greater kudu — one of Africa's most magnificent antelopes — graze the forest edges. Leopard are present but secretive. The crater lakes attract bushbuck, common waterbuck, buffalo, and large concentrations of waterbirds. The surrounding reserve supports Beisa oryx, Grevy's zebra, reticulated giraffe, and Somali ostrich in the drier habitat zones.`
