      highlights: {
        title: 'Wildlife Highlights — The Migration, Lions & the Great Mara Ecosystem',
        description: `The Masai Mara's defining wildlife event is the annual Wildebeest Migration — the largest terrestrial animal migration on Earth, in which over 1.5 million wildebeest and hundreds of thousands of zebra and Thomson's gazelle move north from the Serengeti to the Mara grasslands between July and October, culminating in the legendary Mara River crossings. The resident lion population is the reserve's year-round defining wildlife feature — multiple large prides including the BBC-documented Marsh Pride, Olare Pride, and Paradise Pride roam territories across the reserve with a combined population density that makes reliable sightings virtually guaranteed on any game drive. Resident cheetah coalitions and females with dependent cubs are encountered regularly on the open Mara plains; the Mara consistently supports one of the finest cheetah populations in East Africa. Leopard are reliably found in the riverine fig forest along the Mara River and Talek River corridors. The Mara River itself supports enormous pods of hippo and one of the densest Nile crocodile populations in Africa. Elephant herds, buffalo, topi, hartebeest, impala, and the full suite of Mara prey species complete an ecosystem of extraordinary vitality and density.`
      },
      bestTime: {
        title: 'Best Time for Wildlife Viewing at Masai Mara National Reserve',
        description: `The Masai Mara delivers exceptional wildlife year-round. July to October is peak season, combining the Wildebeest Migration with the best wildlife concentrations and dry-season conditions. The river crossings — which occur unpredictably and at different points along the Mara River — are most reliable from mid-July to mid-October; August and September are the peak crossing months. Lion viewing is outstanding in every month. January to March delivers excellent predator activity, abundant newborn prey, and the least vehicle congestion of any productive season.`
      },
