        description: `Meru National Park's wildlife is anchored by its healthy elephant population — large herds that congregate along the Tana River during the dry season — and its successfully re-established white rhino population in the dedicated sanctuary. Cheetah are encountered regularly in the open savannah zones and are among the most reliably seen in Kenya outside the Masai Mara ecosystem. Resident lion prides with well-established territories across the park provide reliable sightings for the patient visitor. Leopard inhabit the riverine forest corridors along the Tana and Rojewero. The park's extraordinary ecological range — from arid thornbush to lush riverine forest — supports a correspondingly wide ungulate diversity: reticulated giraffe, Beisa oryx, lesser kudu, common waterbuck, topi, and large herds of common zebra. The Rojewero River is the most scenically beautiful wildlife-viewing corridor in the park, with excellent hippo and elephant encounters in a landscape of extraordinary natural beauty.`
