      mostHelpfulExpert: {
        id: 1, author: 'Dr. Amelia Ngcobo', park: 'ZA', parkFlag: 'za',
        credentials: 'Conservation geneticist, rhino specialist, Oryx journal contributor.',
        title: 'The Greatest Rhino Conservation Achievement in Africa',
        rating: 5, date: 'October 2025',
        excerpt: 'No private wildlife area in Kenya — perhaps in all of Africa — combines the depth of conservation achievement, the quality of wildlife experience, and the significance of its living wildlife heritage in the way that Ol Pejeta does. The black rhino programme is without peer. The northern white rhino are irreplaceable. The big cat viewing is outstanding. And the chimpanzee sanctuary is one of the strangest and most moving wildlife encounters available anywhere on the continent.',
        fullReview: 'No private wildlife area in Kenya — perhaps in all of Africa — combines the depth of conservation achievement, the quality of wildlife experience, and the significance of its living wildlife heritage in the way that Ol Pejeta does. The black rhino programme is without peer: 120-plus individuals, multiple generations of successful breeding, and a security operation that has kept this population alive when every other East African rhino population was being devastated. The northern white rhino are irreplaceable: Najin and Fatu are the last of their kind and their future, however it unfolds, will be determined by the scientists and rangers at Ol Pejeta. The big cat viewing — lion, leopard, cheetah — is outstanding. And the chimpanzee sanctuary, with rescued individuals from west and central Africa living on the equatorial savannah, is one of the strangest and most moving wildlife encounters available anywhere on the continent.',
        helpful: 198, avatar: null
      },
      latestUserReview: {
        id: 1, author: 'Keiko Yamamoto', park: 'JP', parkFlag: 'jp',
        title: 'Black rhino tracking on foot was the most thrilling experience of my entire safari life',
        rating: 5, date: 'August 14, 2025',
        excerpt: 'Mother black rhino with calf at 30 metres on foot. Total silence. The ranger between us and her. Twelve safaris. Nothing compares.',
        fullReview: 'Our tracker located a mother black rhino with a six-month calf in thick acacia bush on the second morning. We left the vehicle and approached on foot in absolute silence to within thirty metres. The ranger was positioned between us and her — calm, completely assured, in total control. She looked up at us once, assessed us for perhaps ten seconds, and returned to feeding. We stood there for twenty minutes in the Laikipia morning watching one of the rarest animals in the world going about its life. I have been on twelve safaris in seven countries over fifteen years. Nothing — absolutely nothing — compares to standing on foot watching a wild black rhino with her calf at thirty metres in open African bush.',
        helpful: 167, verified: true, tourTaken: 'Ol Pejeta & Lewa Rhino Safari — 5 Days', avatar: null
      }
