      highlights: {
        title: 'Wildlife Highlights — Black Rhino, Northern White Rhino & Big Five',
        description: `Ol Pejeta Conservancy's defining wildlife asset is its black rhino population — over 120 individuals making it East Africa's largest single black rhino sanctuary. Multiple rhino are reliably encountered on every game drive, and guided foot tracking encounters with known individuals — conducted with armed rangers who know each rhino personally — are among the most thrilling wildlife experiences available anywhere in Kenya. The northern white rhino enclosure houses Najin and Fatu — the last two surviving individuals of the northern white rhinoceros subspecies — whose conservation represents one of the most urgent and extraordinary stories in wildlife protection. African wild dog packs are resident at Ol Pejeta — one of the few Laikipia conservancies with a reliable resident pack. Lion prides, resident leopard, cheetah, and elephant herds complete the Big Five experience. The Ol Pejeta chimpanzee sanctuary — housing rescued chimpanzees from central Africa — is one of the most unusual wildlife experiences in Kenya, watching primates adapted to life on equatorial savannah in a setting unlike any other chimp habitat in Africa.`
      },
