      highlights: {
        title: 'Wildlife Highlights — Sitatunga, De Brazza\'s Monkey & Spotted-necked Otter',
        description: `Saiwa Swamp National Park's defining wildlife trio — the sitatunga antelope, De Brazza's monkey, and spotted-necked otter — are each among Kenya's rarest and most localised mammals, and all three are reliably encountered on guided morning walks in the park. The sitatunga is the park's star: a medium-sized forest antelope with elongated, splayed hooves perfectly adapted to life in papyrus swamp, wading at the water's edge in early morning and evening. Males carry elegant spiral horns and a shaggy, dark chestnut coat; females are redder and hornless. They are most reliably seen from the upper and lower observation platforms from 6–8am and 4–6pm. De Brazza's monkey — among the most strikingly beautiful primates in Africa, with a white beard, chestnut brow patch, and contrasting black and grey coat — inhabits the riverine forest along the Saiwa River and are reliably encountered in small family groups. The spotted-necked otter plays and hunts in the clear Saiwa River sections visible from the boardwalk. Black-and-white colobus and red-tailed monkeys inhabit the canopy forest. Marsh mongoose and a range of smaller mammals complete a mammal community of extraordinary intimacy and rarity.`
      },
      bestTime: {
        title: 'Best Time for Wildlife Viewing at Saiwa Swamp National Park',
        description: `Saiwa is most rewarding year-round but peaks during the dry season from June to October when vegetation is reduced and sitatunga are most visible at the swamp margins. Early morning visits — arriving at the gate by 6am — are essential for sitatunga and bird viewing; sitatunga are most active from 6–8am and 4–6pm. De Brazza's monkey and colobus viewing is excellent in all months. January to March offers very good conditions as a dry-season second window.`
      },
