      highlights: {
        title: 'Wildlife Highlights — The Northern Five, Predators & Volcanic Springs',
        description: `Shaba National Reserve\'s defining wildlife experience is the encounter with Kenya\'s northern specialist species in settings of volcanic drama. Grevy\'s zebra — the world\'s largest and most endangered wild zebra — are found reliably in the open plains and lava fields; they are strikingly different from common zebra in their narrower stripes, larger ears, and white belly, and are globally restricted to northern Kenya and southern Ethiopia. Reticulated giraffe — the most graphically marked of all giraffe subspecies, with a coat of large cream-bordered polygons — are common throughout the reserve. Beisa oryx stand sentinel on the lava plains; gerenuk browse upright on their hind legs in the acacia thickets. The Ewaso Nyiro River, forming the reserve's northern boundary, supports large elephant herds, Nile crocodile, and one of northern Kenya's most reliably seen leopard populations in the dense riparian woodland. Lion prides are present, and cheetah hunt in the open lava-plain areas. Shaba's crystal springs — Shaba Spring and Penny's Drop, named for Joy Adamson's leopard — are extraordinary focal points where wildlife congregates at clear blue-green pools in the midst of dry bush.`
      },
