          fullReview: 'I have worked in the Taita Hills for sixteen years. The three endemic birds — Taita thrush, Taita apalis, Taita white-eye — are among the rarest and most globally significant species in Kenya. The Taita thrush\'s total world population is approximately 1,350 individuals, all confined to the tiny forest fragments of the Taita Hills. Finding one requires local knowledge, patience, and an early start. But the context makes it extraordinary: you arrive the evening before at the Salt Lick Lodge, watch elephant move through the floodlit waterhole below your elevated pod, wake before dawn, drive across the Highland moorland with lion and buffalo, then walk into the ancient Afromontane forest at sunrise for the thrushes. That combination — Big Five safari and globally threatened endemic birds in a landscape of extraordinary scenic beauty — is unavailable anywhere else in Kenya, or possibly Africa.',
