          excerpt: 'Four hundred elephant at Mudanda Rock. Three other vehicles in the entire park. The scale, the red dust, the silence. The Africa of the history books, still intact.',
          fullReview: 'I have been visiting African national parks for fifteen years. I came to Tsavo East because I wanted the real thing — not a theme park safari, not a curated experience, but Africa as it actually is. We arrived at Mudanda Rock at 6:30am on a Saturday in August. Four hundred elephant were at and around the natural waterhole below the rock. Some were on the rock itself, their red dust-coated hides vivid against the grey granite. There were three other vehicles in the entire surrounding area. The only sounds were the rumbling of the herd, the occasional splash, and the birds along the river. We stayed for two hours. In fifteen years of African safari — the Serengeti, the Okavango, Kruger, Etosha — that morning at Mudanda Rock in August was the finest single wildlife experience of my life. The scale, the colour, the solitude. If you understand what makes Africa extraordinary, you will understand why Tsavo East is irreplaceable.',
