        title: 'Wildlife Highlights — Red Elephants, Mudanda Rock & the Galana River',
        description: `Tsavo East National Park's defining wildlife experience is its elephant — the largest elephant herds remaining in Kenya, their coats red from the park's iron-rich laterite dust, moving across the vast semi-arid plains in numbers that have been described since the time of the early explorers. Tsavo once held the largest elephant population in the world before the ivory poaching crisis of the 1970s–1980s; numbers have recovered significantly and current estimates place the park's population at over 12,000 animals. The Galana River — the park's permanent water source — concentrates lion, leopard, elephant, Nile crocodile, and hippo along its 160km course through the park. Mudanda Rock, a 1.6km inselberg that creates a natural dam, gathers elephant in their hundreds from August to October for one of the most spectacular wildlife viewing opportunities in East Africa. The northern sections of Tsavo East approach the Northern Frontier District biome, adding northern species — Grevy's zebra, gerenuk, Beisa oryx, reticulated giraffe — to the lion, leopard, and elephant core of the southern areas. Tsavo's black rhino — devastated by poaching in the 1970s–1980s — are recovering under intensive protection in sections of the park.`
