      highlights: {
        title: 'Wildlife Highlights — Grumeti River Crossings, Resident Predators & Exclusive Western Serengeti Access',
        description: `Grumeti Game Reserve's most dramatic wildlife event is the Great Migration crossing of the Grumeti River — one of the three great river crossings of the annual wildebeest migration. The Grumeti River holds some of the largest Nile crocodile in the Serengeti ecosystem, many decades old and of extraordinary size, which ambush crossing wildebeest columns from June to July. Unlike the Mara River crossings in Kenya, which attract hundreds of vehicles, the Grumeti crossings take place within the exclusive private reserve and may be observed by only the guests of a single camp. The permanent resident wildlife of Grumeti — independent of migration timing — is exceptional. Multiple lion prides hold exclusive territories within the reserve; large leopard males are well-known to the Singita guiding team and reliably encountered on night drives. Cheetah hunt the open grassland areas. Elephant bulls and small family groups are common throughout. The Grumeti River — flowing through the reserve year-round — concentrates hippo, crocodile, and a wealth of waterbirds in its pools and rapids. Roan antelope — an uncommon and sought-after species across the broader Serengeti ecosystem — are reliably found in the reserve's western woodland zones.`
      },
