          fullReview: 'I have spent twenty years in Katavi. I was born in western Tanzania and I have guided in Katavi since I was old enough to hold a radio. I have also guided in the Serengeti, Selous, Ruaha, and Mahale. None of those parks — which are all extraordinary — do what Katavi does in September. The hippo pool at Chada in late September: four hundred animals, the water almost gone, fighting for space in slow-motion battles that produce wounds you cannot believe a living animal can sustain. The buffalo herds crossing the plain at first light: four hundred, five hundred animals in a column that takes twenty minutes to pass. And the lion that follow them — not hiding, not shy, but walking openly behind the herd in a relationship of predator and prey that looks exactly like what it is: the most fundamental dynamic in African ecology, undisturbed and undiluted. I have never had a visitor leave Katavi without saying it was the most powerful wildlife experience of their life. I believe them. It is the most powerful wildlife experience of mine too, after twenty years.',
