      highlights: {
        title: 'Wildlife Highlights — Tree-Climbing Lions, Flamingo Spectacle & Groundwater Forest Elephants',
        description: `Lake Manyara National Park's most celebrated wildlife features are its tree-climbing lion and its flamingo concentrations. The tree-climbing behaviour has been documented at Lake Manyara for decades — prides regularly rest in the branches of large Acacia tortilis and Kigelia (sausage) trees, particularly in the late morning as temperatures rise; the behaviour is believed to relate to insect avoidance and the cooling effect of the breeze at elevation. Sightings are not guaranteed but are reliable enough — particularly from July to October when the prides are most active in the floodplain woodland — that tree-climbing lions remain one of the park's defining attractions. The alkaline lake holds seasonal flamingo concentrations that in good years number several hundred thousand birds — the alkaline chemistry of the water supports the blue-green algae on which flamingo feed. Lesser flamingo dominate but greater flamingo are also present. Water level variations significantly affect flamingo numbers from year to year; current conditions should be confirmed before a visit centred on the flamingo spectacle. The groundwater forest — a continuous strip of giant fig, mahogany, and wild date palm running along the escarpment base — holds large elephant family groups, enormous baboon troops, and blue monkey in one of the most accessible forest wildlife experiences in Tanzania. Over 400 bird species are recorded across the park's three primary habitats — groundwater forest, alkaline lake, and floodplain grassland — making Lake Manyara one of the finest birding parks on the Tanzania northern circuit.`
      },
      bestTime: {
        title: 'Best Time for Wildlife Viewing at Lake Manyara National Park',
        description: `June to October is the optimal period for tree-climbing lion sightings (when prides are most active on the floodplain), flamingo concentrations (most reliable in the dry season when lake salinity is highest), and the forest elephant encounters (when herds concentrate around groundwater sources). January and February are an excellent secondary dry-season window. The park delivers good wildlife viewing year-round — even in the wet season the forest is extraordinarily active and elephant encounters in the fig forest are outstanding. The flamingo spectacle is the most variable element and depends on lake water levels.`
      },
