      description: `Mahale Mountains National Park's defining wildlife experience is chimpanzee trekking with the habituated M-group. This community of approximately 60 individuals has been studied and habituated to human presence since 1965, making them one of the most behaviourally approachable wild chimpanzee groups anywhere in Africa. Trekking typically involves two to four hours of forest walking following the group's daily ranging movements; once located, visitors spend up to one hour with the chimpanzees at close range. Encounters vary from brief sightings to extended sessions of social grooming, feeding, play, and inter-group vocalisations. Red colobus, red-tailed monkey, blue monkey, and olive baboon are all commonly encountered in the forest on trekking routes. Hippopotamus are present along the Lake Tanganyika shore. The lake itself — one of the world's great freshwater bodies — holds over 250 endemic cichlid species visible while snorkelling in the crystal-clear water directly from the lodge beach. Forest birding is excellent, with multiple Congo Basin species at the easternmost edge of their range.`
