    highlights: {
      title: 'Wildlife Highlights — Lion Prides, Elephant Herds & the Mkata Floodplain',
      description: `Mikumi National Park's prime wildlife habitat is the Mkata River floodplain — a broad, open grassland that delivers some of Tanzania's most accessible open-country game viewing south of the Serengeti. Lion prides are the floodplain's most sought-after sighting; several resident prides hunt zebra, wildebeest, and buffalo on the open plain and are regularly found resting in the acacia woodland at the floodplain margins in the morning. Elephant herds are large and numerous — Mikumi holds some of the most reliable and approachable elephant encounters in southern Tanzania, with families regularly encountered on the floodplain tracks at close range. The hippo pools at the Mkata River hold good numbers of hippopotamus accessible from the road. Giraffe, zebra, wildebeest, and impala are abundant on the floodplain. African wild dog packs transit through Mikumi as part of their wide-ranging movement through the Selous-Mikumi ecosystem; sightings are opportunistic rather than reliable. Over 400 bird species include yellow-throated longclaw, bateleur, Miombo rock thrush, and a full complement of floodplain and wetland species.`
    },
