    highlights: {
      title: 'Wildlife Highlights — Black Rhino Sanctuary, Wild Dog & Dry-Country Species',
      description: `Mkomazi National Park's most distinctive wildlife feature is its black rhinoceros sanctuary — a carefully managed protected area holding some of Tanzania's critically endangered black rhino population. Sanctuary visits are conducted in small groups with a TANAPA ranger escort and must be booked in advance; they provide one of East Africa's most remarkable conservation-focused wildlife encounters. The African wild dog captive breeding and reintroduction programme, initiated by the George Adamson Wildlife Preservation Trust, represents one of Africa's most significant wild dog conservation projects. Wild dog sightings in the broader park are possible for visitors with time and patience. The semi-arid landscape supports a suite of dry-country species characteristic of the Tsavo ecosystem — gerenuk, lesser kudu, fringe-eared oryx, and Grant's gazelle — that distinguish Mkomazi from any other park on the Tanzania northern circuit. Elephant, lion, cheetah, and giraffe are present across the semi-arid plains. The Kilimanjaro views from the park's northern sector are among the finest in East Africa.`
    },
    bestTime: {
      title: 'Best Time for Wildlife Viewing at Mkomazi National Park',
      description: `June to October is optimal — dry conditions make the semi-arid vegetation most open, wildlife concentrates at seasonal waterholes, and the Kilimanjaro views are clearest in the dry air. Black rhino sanctuary visits and wild dog tracking can be arranged year-round but dry season conditions make access and wildlife location most reliable.`
    },
