        title: 'Black rhino, four lion prides, hippos, flamingos — the most extraordinary day of wildlife in my life',
        rating: 5, date: 'August 3, 2025',
        excerpt: 'Eleven hours in the Ngorongoro Crater. Black rhino by 7:30am. Three lion prides by 9am. Flamingos on Lake Magadi. The buffalo herd stretching to the crater wall. Nothing could have prepared me for it.',
        fullReview: 'We allocated one full day to the Ngorongoro Crater, descending at first light and ascending at last light. Our guide Joseph had the crater floor mapped in his head — where the rhino were ranging, where the morning lion prides would be resting after the night, which route to take first. By 7:30am we had found a black rhino — a large cow moving alone through the short grassland near the western rim descent. By 9am we had found three separate lion prides. The eastern pride had killed a wildebeest during the night and were still feeding. The flamingo concentration on Lake Magadi was extraordinary — thousands of birds in an unbroken pink line against the crater wall. The Ngoitoktok hippo pool, the buffalo herd, the hyena den with cubs. Eleven hours that I have replayed in my mind every day since returning home. Ngorongoro is unlike anything else in Africa. It is one of the greatest natural experiences on Earth.',
