    highlights: {
      title: 'Wildlife Highlights — Elephant on the River, Wild Dog at the Den, and Lion on the Plains',
      description: `Ruaha National Park's defining wildlife feature is its elephant population — Tanzania's largest, with over 10,000 animals that concentrate along the Great Ruaha River in the dry season in herds of extraordinary size and in complete absence of crowds. The Great Ruaha River is the park's wildlife engine: as the surrounding Miombo woodland dries from June to October, all species funnel to its permanent pools and sandy banks — elephant, hippo, crocodile, lion, buffalo, and an extraordinary diversity of waterbirds all converging on the same narrow corridor. Lion prides at Ruaha are among the largest in Tanzania, with some exceeding 20 individuals; sightings are reliable and often extended. Wild dog are present and most reliably encountered during the denning season from May to July, when packs with pups can be visited over multiple mornings at active dens. Leopard are well-seen in the rocky kopje country and along the riverine forest. The combination of these five key species — elephant, lion, wild dog, leopard, and cheetah — in a single uncrowded park makes Ruaha the finest all-round predator and megafauna destination in the Tanzania southern circuit.`
    },
