    highlights: {
      title: 'Wildlife Highlights — Eight Primate Species and the Community Conservation Story',
      description: `Bigodi Wetland Sanctuary's defining wildlife feature is the opportunity to encounter up to eight primate species on a single 3-hour guided walk — an experience available at very few sites in East Africa. Red colobus, black-and-white colobus, L'Hoest's monkey, red-tailed monkey, blue monkey, and olive baboon are reliably seen on most walks; grey-cheeked mangabey and chimpanzee are encountered less reliably but regularly enough to make them genuine targets. The sanctuary's papyrus swamp, fig forest edge, and canopy forest transition create a multi-habitat experience that is also exceptional for birds — the Papyrus Gonolek and Great Blue Turaco are the headline species but the full list runs to over 200 species on the wetland walk alone. The community conservation story is inseparable from the wildlife experience at Bigodi: the sanctuary exists because the local community chose to protect it, and every visiting fee directly funds education and healthcare in the surrounding villages. This context gives the Bigodi experience a meaning and resonance that purely commercial wildlife destinations cannot provide.`
    },
