        credentials: 'Bugoma Forest Reserve guide and conservation advocate, 8 years. Uganda Bird Guides Club member.',
        title: 'Uganda\'s Last Frontier for Chimpanzee Tourism — Private, Unspoilt, and Under Real Threat',
        rating: 4, visitedTimes: '300+ times',
        date: 'September 15, 2025',
        excerpt: 'Bugoma has what Kibale had before it became famous. Silence. Privacy. Chimpanzees that are not surrounded by cameras. A forest that still feels like a secret. Come before it changes.',
        fullReview: 'I started guiding at Bugoma in 2017 after working briefly at Kibale. The difference was immediate. At Kibale in peak season, a chimpanzee tracking permit puts you in a group of eight tourists following a habituated group that has been followed every day for thirty years. At Bugoma, a tracking permit often puts you alone — just you and your guide — following a group that has been habituated more recently and that still retains a quality of wildness that you cannot find in the well-worn forests. In eight years of guiding at Bugoma, I have never had two groups of tourists in the forest at the same time. The chimpanzee encounters are entirely private. They last as long as the chimpanzees allow, not as long as a schedule dictates. Bugoma is also a serious birding destination — the Albertine Rift specials here include some species more reliably found here than at Kibale or Budongo. The forest is under threat from agricultural expansion and the conservation situation has been difficult in recent years. Every visitor who comes to Bugoma helps demonstrate that the forest has economic value as a living wilderness. Come.',
