      title: 'Off the beaten track and completely worth it — Bugoma was the most surprising and rewarding detour of our Uganda trip',
      rating: 4, date: 'December 28, 2025',
      excerpt: 'We tracked chimpanzees at three sites in Uganda — Kibale, Budongo, and Bugoma. Bugoma was the most private and the most moving. Completely alone in the forest for three hours. The conservation context made it feel important.',
      fullReview: 'We visited Bugoma on the recommendation of our operator as a half-day detour between Murchison Falls and Kibale. I had done no research on Bugoma before arriving — I barely knew where it was. Our guide Moses collected us from Hoima at 6am. By 7:30am we were in the forest. By 8:15am we were sitting with a group of approximately 20 chimpanzees in a clearing among large mahogany trees. We were completely alone — no other tourists, no other guides, no vehicles. The encounter lasted two hours. The chimpanzees moved, fed, groomed, called, and played entirely on their own terms without any awareness of the schedule. Moses explained the conservation situation as we walked back — the pressure on the forest, the legal battles, the community partnerships. The experience felt consequential in a way that the more established sites cannot fully replicate. For adventurous and conservation-minded Uganda travellers, Bugoma is an essential addition to the western circuit.',
