    bestTimeForBirdWatching: {
      description: `Bwindi is Uganda's most important single location for Albertine Rift endemic birds — 23 endemics and near-endemics are found here that occur in no other Uganda park. The Ruhija sector is the outstanding birding area: the montane forest, bamboo zone, and highland bogs of Ruhija deliver African Green Broadbill, Grauer\'s Rush Warbler, Short-tailed Warbler, Kivu Ground Thrush, Chapin\'s Flycatcher, and Ruwenzori Batis. The Buhoma sector adds Bar-tailed Trogon, Black-billed Turaco, and Strange Weaver. June to August and December to February are the best months for endemic birding — clearest visibility in drier forest conditions. November to April brings Palearctic migrants. Dedicated Albertine Rift birding itineraries should allocate at least two full days to Ruhija. Bwindi birding is most rewarding when combined with birding at Queen Elizabeth National Park and Kibale for a complete Uganda Albertine Rift endemic list.`
    }
