  reviewsContent: {
    expertRating: { overall: 4.5, totalReviews: 8, categories: { wildlife: 4.4, scenery: 4.5, bushVibe: 4.6, birding: 4.7 } },
    userRating: { overall: 4.5, totalReviews: 312, categories: { wildlife: 4.4, scenery: 4.5, bushVibe: 4.6, birding: 4.6 } },
    allExpertReviews: [
      {
        id: 1,
        author: 'Moses Tugume',
        park: 'UG', parkFlag: 'ug',
        credentials: 'Uganda Wildlife Authority-accredited forest guide, 11 years at Kalinzu Forest Reserve and Queen Elizabeth National Park.',
        title: 'Kalinzu is the Best Kept Secret in Western Uganda — Habituated Chimpanzees, Nine Primates, Albertine Rift Endemics, and Almost No Other Visitors',
        rating: 5, visitedTimes: '500+ guided visits',
        date: 'September 3, 2025',
        excerpt: 'I have guided chimpanzee treks at Kalinzu for eleven years. Most guests arrive expecting something lesser than Kibale. What they find is a forest that is older, quieter, and wilder — a habituated chimpanzee community that has never been overwhelmed by visitor numbers, and Albertine Rift endemic birds that can be found in the first hour of a morning walk.',
        fullReview: 'Kalinzu is the forest that the operators who know Uganda best keep recommending. It is 137km² of ancient moist forest — older growth, in some areas, than anything at Kibale. The chimpanzees were habituated in the 1990s and the community of 60–70 individuals is well-established and relaxed around people. What makes Kalinzu different is the absence of crowds. On most days there are one or two trekking groups in the entire forest. You find the chimpanzees and they are yours. You hear them in the canopy before you see them and your guide knows their patterns well enough that you are almost always with them within forty minutes of entering the forest. The birding at Kalinzu is something that serious birders discover and never stop talking about. 375 species in a forest this small is a remarkable density. African Green Broadbill, Collared Apalis, Yellow-eyed Black Flycatcher, Grauer\'s Warbler — a specialist birder can accumulate an extraordinary list in two days here. And the forest sits ten minutes south of Queen Elizabeth National Park. Guests can have chimpanzees in the morning and a boat cruise on the Kazinga Channel in the afternoon. It is one of the finest single-day contrasts in Uganda.',
        helpful: 94, categories: { wildlife: 4.4, scenery: 4.5, bushVibe: 4.6, birding: 4.7 }
      }
    ],
    allUserReviews: [
      {
        id: 1,
        author: 'Pieter van der Merwe',
        park: 'ZA', parkFlag: 'za',
        title: 'Chimpanzees to ourselves for forty minutes — Kalinzu is what Uganda\'s forest safaris used to feel like before the crowds arrived',
        rating: 5, date: 'August 14, 2025',
        excerpt: 'We added Kalinzu at our operator\'s suggestion as a Queen Elizabeth add-on. Two nights, one chimpanzee trek, one early morning bird walk. The chimp community was found in twenty minutes and we were the only group there for the next forty. That never happens at Kibale.',
        fullReview: 'We had three nights at Queen Elizabeth — boat cruise, morning game drives in the Ishasha sector for tree-climbing lions, the usual superb circuit. Our operator quietly suggested two nights at Kalinzu as an add-on, saying the chimpanzee permit price was a fraction of Kibale and the forest was better quality. They were right. We entered the forest at 8am. Our guide Moses — who has been working this forest for eleven years — tracked the chimps by sound for about fifteen minutes and then we were there. A group of seven individuals, two with juveniles, moving slowly through the mid-storey about four metres above our heads. Nobody else came. For forty minutes we stood in that ancient forest with those chimpanzees and nothing interrupted us. At Kibale on a peak season day you might share that moment with twenty other tourists and three guides. At Kalinzu it was just us. That quietness changes the experience entirely. The forest itself is spectacular — very tall canopy, immense old figs, the constant sound of birdsong. Our guide identified African Green Broadbill on the walk back to camp. It was one of the best mornings of any African trip I have taken.',
        helpful: 127, verified: true, tourTaken: 'Queen Elizabeth & Kalinzu Forest 6 Days'
      }
    ],
    mostHelpfulExpert: {
      id: 1, author: 'Moses Tugume', park: 'UG', parkFlag: 'ug',
      credentials: 'Uganda Wildlife Authority-accredited forest guide, 11 years Kalinzu Forest Reserve.',
      title: 'Habituated Chimpanzees, Nine Primate Species, Albertine Rift Endemics — and Nobody Else in the Forest',
      rating: 5, date: 'September 2025',
      excerpt: 'Kalinzu is older forest than Kibale in most areas and quieter on any given day. The chimpanzees are well-habituated and the birding is extraordinary. This is the forest that Ugandans who love Uganda recommend.',
      helpful: 94, avatar: null
    },
    latestUserReview: {
      id: 2, author: 'Claire Moreau', park: 'FR', parkFlag: 'fr',
      title: 'African Green Broadbill and Collared Apalis before breakfast — for an Albertine Rift birder Kalinzu is an unmissable forest',
      rating: 5, date: 'January 10, 2026',
      excerpt: 'I scheduled two days at Kalinzu specifically for the Albertine Rift endemics and the forest delivered everything I had hoped for. 375 species in 137km² — the density here is remarkable, and the quiet forest means you can actually hear everything.',
      fullReview: 'I had been to Kibale and Bwindi on previous Uganda trips and specifically added Kalinzu on this visit for the Albertine Rift endemics that are reportedly easier here due to lower visitor pressure. Day one: African Green Broadbill in the first hour, Collared Apalis forty minutes later, Yellow-eyed Black Flycatcher before breakfast. My guide was one of the best forest birders I have encountered in any African forest — calm, precise, with an extraordinary ear for the subtle differences between similar warbler calls in the understorey. By the end of day two I had recorded 68 species for the reserve, including six Albertine Rift endemics and several species I had dipped on at Bwindi due to the heavier visitor pressure there. The chimpanzee trek on day one was a bonus that turned into a highlight — the community was found in a fig tree in the northern sector and stayed in that tree for over an hour while we stood beneath them. Kalinzu should be on every serious birder\'s Uganda itinerary. It is genuinely outstanding.',
      helpful: 83, verified: true, tourTaken: 'Uganda Albertine Rift Birding 10 Days', avatar: null
    }
  },

