    highlights: {
      title: 'Wildlife Highlights — Chimpanzees, Nine Primates, and Albertine Rift Birds in an Ancient Forest',
      description: `Kalinzu Forest Reserve's principal wildlife attraction is its habituated chimpanzee community — approximately 60–70 individuals that have been conditioned to tolerate human presence since the 1990s. Daily chimpanzee trekking permits are available at a price well below Kibale's, and because visitor numbers are far lower, trekking groups are rarely competing for access. The forest itself — ancient moist semi-deciduous, with tall fig trees and a dense understorey — is one of the finest in Uganda for primate diversity: nine species in 137km², including grey-cheeked mangabey, L'Hoest's monkey, and red colobus alongside the more visible red-tailed, blue, and colobus species. The birding at Kalinzu is the reserve's second major draw: 375 species recorded, with multiple Albertine Rift endemics well-represented, particularly in the deeper forest and along the river courses.`
    },
    bestTime: {
      title: 'Best Time for Chimpanzee Trekking at Kalinzu',
      description: `June to August and December to February are the recommended months for chimpanzee trekking at Kalinzu — drier paths make forest movement easier and tracking more manageable. Chimpanzees are present and trackable year-round. The wet season (March to May, October to November) brings muddy conditions and reduced tracking success rates on some days, but also quieter forests, lower permit availability competition, and the reserve at its most spectacularly green. Birding is outstanding year-round.`
    },
